# How to change the wiretap-appliance unit without editing it.
#
# **This file is chassis-owned and is rewritten by every `packaging render`.**
# It is also a `.sample` rather than a `.conf`, so systemd ignores it even if
# the whole directory is installed — copy it to a file named for what it does,
# `10-serial-device.conf` say, and edit that. Not `10-local.conf`: that is the
# name an operator reaches for under /etc/systemd/system/, where their file
# would mask a shipped one of the same name wholesale. Files you add here are
# never written by the chassis, which is
# the whole arrangement: the unit beside this directory is regenerated, and
# everything a product wants to say about it lives in here instead. **Every
# `*.conf` in this directory ships**: `make-deb.sh` stages them into
# `/usr/lib/systemd/system/wiretap-appliance.service.d/`, and this sample stays behind.
#
# Drop-ins are read in lexical order after the unit itself, so `10-` before
# `20-`, and the last word wins for the directives where winning is the model.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three kinds of directive, and they do not behave the same way
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the part that catches people, because a drop-in looks like an
# override and is only sometimes one.
#
# **1. A scalar is last-wins.** `Restart=`, `User=`, `PrivateDevices=` and the
# rest of the booleans. Assigning it again is all there is to it:
#
#     [Service]
#     PrivateDevices=no
#
# **2. A list accumulates.** `Environment=`, `ReadWritePaths=`, `DeviceAllow=`,
# `RestrictAddressFamilies=`, `CapabilityBoundingSet=`. A second assignment adds
# to the first rather than replacing it, so this *widens* the unit:
#
#     [Service]
#     ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/wiretap-appliance-spool
#
# **3. A list you want to replace has to be reset first**, with one empty
# assignment, and then set. Without the reset you get the union of both, which
# for an address-family or capability list means the sandbox you were trying to
# narrow is still as wide as it was:
#
#     [Service]
#     RestrictAddressFamilies=
#     RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_NETLINK AF_PACKET
#
# **`ExecStart=` is the one where getting this wrong stops the unit.** It is a
# list, and unless `Type=` is `oneshot` systemd refuses more than one entry —
# which this unit's `Type=exec` is not. So a drop-in that assigns it without the
# reset is a unit that fails to start rather than one that quietly does the
# wrong thing. That failure is the good case.
#
#     [Service]
#     ExecStart=
#     ExecStart=/usr/bin/wiretap-appliance --some-flag
#
# Prefer not to. `ExecStart` is a bare binary on purpose — every path it could
# name is already resolved from the unit's own directories — and a flag added
# here is a flag `config.toml` should be answering.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a drop-in cannot do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# **Remove a dependency.** systemd.unit(5) is explicit that `After=`, `Wants=`
# and the rest cannot be reset to an empty list — so this unit's
# `network-online.target` can be added to and never taken off. Dropping one
# means overriding the whole unit, which is the thing this arrangement exists
# to avoid.
#
# That is the real limit, and it is worth separating from one that looks like
# it and is not: a boolean the chassis sets, such as `ProtectClock=yes`, *can*
# be undone — `ProtectClock=no` is the default and puts it back exactly. So
# "the chassis set this and I cannot unset it" applies to dependencies, not to
# the sandbox.
#
# If a product genuinely needs the chassis to stop emitting something, that is
# a knob missing from `appliance.toml` and a change to this repository, not a
# workaround here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A worked example: an appliance with a serial-attached device
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to `10-serial-device.conf`, keeping only the lines you need.
#
#     [Service]
#     # A scalar: last-wins, no reset.
#     PrivateDevices=no
#     # A list: accumulates, so this adds to whatever the unit already allows.
#     DeviceAllow=/dev/ttyUSB0 rw
#     # A list being narrowed rather than widened, so it is reset first.
#     RestrictAddressFamilies=
#     RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_NETLINK
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One caveat, stated rather than glossed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# **systemd.syntax(7) is explicit that whether a setting accumulates is
# per-setting, and is documented on the setting or not at all.** Of the ones
# named above, systemd.exec(5) documents the merge for `CapabilityBoundingSet=`
# ("the bounding sets are merged by OR"), `AmbientCapabilities=`,
# `Environment=` and `ReadWritePaths=`. For `RestrictAddressFamilies=` it
# documents only the empty-string reset — so the accumulate half of that one is
# observed behaviour rather than a promise. `DeviceAllow=` is
# systemd.resource-control(5), not systemd.exec(5).
#
# None of it has been verified on a real board by anyone working on this
# chassis. Check the unit you actually get with `systemctl cat
# wiretap-appliance.service`, and the sandbox you actually get with `systemd-analyze
# security wiretap-appliance.service`, before hanging a security property off a drop-in.
